every Wednesday our morning rounds looks  at medical issues that could affect you  and discusses solutions for better  health this morning there's a new  government warning about a serious polio  like illness that mainly strikes young  children we are entering peak season for  acute flaccid myelitis also known as afm  so far the CDC says there are 11  confirmed cases in eight states and more  are expected now last year's outbreak  infected a record 233 patients AFM can  cause paralysis and life-threatening  breathing problems we introduced you now  to a seven-year-old sebastian Bottomly  last year the illness paralyzed him and  our doctor new rule is here was more on  this story I guess the question now is  Tara how is Sebastian doing these days  yes well Sebastian's story Gale shows us  the overwhelming impact of this rare  illness he's been through more than  2,000 hours of physical therapy and  still struggles to walk almost three  years after being diagnosed it's been a  long road and it's a still an ongoing  process  Sebastian's mother Krista Bottomly says  her son's journey began in 2016 when his  cold turned into the polio like illness  leaving him paralyzed from the neck down  we were there for Sebastian's fight to  walk again  in 2018 here 'i learned to dress eat and  stand by himself but now learning to  walk again is a slow grueling process  while he's able to move most of his body  after all the work that we've done it is  still a struggle for him to walk he it's  if we have to go distances he mostly was  in the wheelchair since Sebastian became  sick he's been an intensive rehab  everyday he feels very frustrated when  he remembers what he used to be able to  do and now he can't but he's also  feeling excited about what  learn to be able to do it's been a long  journey to get here but I think he's  feeling encouraged and hopeful it's good  to see him Tara good to have you at the  table so let's start with what causes  AFM and what is it spike in the summer  well it's a little bit of a mystery  still you know the CDC is working very  hard to figure that out and the best  theory we have is that it may be caused  by a family of viruses known as the  enterovirus family and so the idea is  that that virus specifically attacks the  motor neurons in the spinal cord that  causes the weakness it also could be an  inflammatory and immune reaction to the  virus that's damaging those nerves but  one of the questions is why does it  affect some kids and not others so if  the virus is in the same family one  sibling might get this and the other one  doesn't so there are a lot of questions  and we're not sure why it has this kind  of biennial pattern where it spikes  every other year we do see it in the  fall and summer months we think again  maybe this is because enterovirus  circulates during that time what are the  symptoms to so symptoms to look for  would be usually children have had an  acute respiratory illness or a fever in  the month preceding and then they  develop sudden onset weakness and  paralysis of the arms or legs they can  also have a facial droop they can have  trouble speaking or swallowing or  difficulty moving their eyes and the big  concern is if it affects the respiratory  muscles they can have trouble breathing  and that can happen very quickly where  they may end up on a ventilator in fact  in this series of numbers the CDC  released about 20 to 30% did require  ventilatory support 60% required  admission to the ICU what's the typical  treatment and what are the long-term  prognosis for these kids well aside from  not understanding the cause we really  don't have a great treatment other than  supportive care and so as we saw in the  piece really intensive physical therapy  and Rehab is kind of the primary  treatment we have tried things like  steroids plasma for rhesus  immunoglobulin therapy but none of those  are really been shown to work so it's a  big question at this point and you know  the long-term recovery also questionmark  some kids have had a long-term paralysis  months or years after this all right  scary stuff Tara thank you very much
 

